SRC/gnuchess -- changes made for the OSK freeware collection  GNUCHESS4.0/SRC/checkgame.c and game.c, 2026-09-26.  Both read the  chess.lst that gnuchess writes when you type `list'.    - Both stopped one move short of the list: the main loop ran      `for (i = 1; i < end; i++)' with end the last move.  It is `<='.    - Their GetGame() counted two moves for every line, so a list that      ends on white's move gained an invented last move; and skip() walked      on past the end of the line looking for a space.  skip() stops at      the end of the line now, and a line with one move counts one.    - game.c cut each board line at line[79], one past the `;' that ends      the board, so every board after the first carried the previous      one's `]' -- `]]'.  It cuts at line[78].  Both rebuilt with gcc2 (they are ANSI C), with NONDSP for no curses,  and checkgame with -mremote for its 86k of move tables -- the flags  the port's own Makefile used.  The originals are checkgame.c.ori and  game.c.ori.  GNUCHESS4.0/SRC/bincheckr.c, 2026-09-26.  bincheckr read an older  binary book than gnuchess 4.0 writes -- a text header, then 16-byte  entries -- so on MISC/gnuchess.data it printed zeroes and then divided  by the zero runs it had counted, and stopped on a math trap.    - It now recognises the 4.0 (GDX) book by its 12-byte header, whose      last field is the offset of the last slot, and reads its 12-byte      entries; the older layout is still read as before.    - With no entries in use it says so instead of dividing by zero.    - Its last line printed the even count as `odd' and the odd as      `even'; they are the right way round.    - With no file named it prints a usage line.  Rebuilt -qm with Microware's cc.  The original is bincheckr.c.ori.